WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.258 --> 00:00:09.800
RETO MEIER: We're joined now by
Adam Seligman, Vice President

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.450
of Developer Relations.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:14.000
Now I love the theme of
last night's keynote,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.392
Get to the Fun Part.

00:00:15.392 --> 00:00:16.850
Can you talk a
little bit about how

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.587
you chose that
theme, and why you

00:00:18.587 --> 00:00:20.170
feel it resonates
with our developers,

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.372
and really all
technical practitioners?

00:00:22.372 --> 00:00:23.580
ADAM SELIGMAN: Yeah, you bet.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.455
Thanks for having me.

00:00:24.455 --> 00:00:26.510
It's exciting to be here.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:27.840
Keynote was super fun.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.410
And the whole idea was
to really celebrate

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.190
what developers and operators
actually do every day.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.940
You know, hopefully
they're spending hours

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.940
a day productively building
apps, running apps, deploying

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.520
apps, monitoring
apps, and not dealing

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.397
with all the other
stuff that you normally

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:44.480
have to do when you build
applications, especially

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.270
in the enterprise.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.630
Getting out of Waterfall
and getting to Agile,

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.240
not just throwing over an app
over the wall for someone else

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.850
to operate, but taking
ownership in a DevOps culture

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.510
and operating, and really
removing all the barriers.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:03.830
Everything from not having to
manage and shard databases, not

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:08.630
have to manually manage your
VMs or even pick VM sizes.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.707
And really just get to the
heart of building applications.

00:01:11.707 --> 00:01:13.790
And then let the Cloud
take care of it from there.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.943
That was the spirit we wanted
to capture in the keynote.

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.610
STEPHANIE WONG: So
configuring networks,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.810
rewriting apps due to
Cloud provider changes

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.940
and waiting around for
build, test and deploy,

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.505
are clearly not the fun parts.

00:01:25.505 --> 00:01:27.380
Didn't Google once face
these sort of issues?

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.050
And how were we able to
remedy that at such scale?

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.430
ADAM SELIGMAN: Google has
built a lot of great software

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.530
to run these services
that billions of people

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.860
use every day.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.520
So a lot of, a couple
of decades of experience

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.250
went into how do we help Google
developers become incredibly

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.690
productive so they can spend
an hour, six hours on average

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.612
of writing software every
day, and absolutely minimize

00:01:47.612 --> 00:01:49.820
everything else in the chain
of getting to deployment

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.980
and getting that service
out to production, where

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.610
a small number of users can
test it as a canary deployment,

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:56.970
and start giving you feedback.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.420
So we're trying to build
that into our Cloud.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.502
We've seen the rise of DevOps
practices out in the industry.

00:02:03.502 --> 00:02:05.210
But now we're building
into our products,

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:08.180
with products like Cloud
Code, like Managed Kubernetes

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:13.310
and CSP, Cloud Run, our
new serverless offering.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.740
Just really reducing
the barriers

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.390
that anyone, like
a Google engineer,

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.520
can build code and
get it in production

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.733
as easily as possible.

00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:21.650
RETO MEIER: Fantastic.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.155
Now if you had to pick just
one, just one best practice

00:02:25.155 --> 00:02:27.530
from yesterday's keynote that
you want developers to walk

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:28.697
away with, what would it be?

00:02:28.697 --> 00:02:30.488
ADAM SELIGMAN: I'm
going to cheat a little,

00:02:30.488 --> 00:02:32.300
and instead of like
I think Google said,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.540
I really like what
Twinkle Desai,

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.040
who is a VP at
Kohl's, who started

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.010
her career as a developer
and a unique sysadmin.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:44.462
And she built a lot of
automation at Kohl's.

00:02:44.462 --> 00:02:45.920
Now she runs, a
couple years later,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.560
she rents a 200-person team.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.560
They have a 100 microservices,
5,000 containers,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.030
it's all automated.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:56.240
It's running on
managed GKE On Prem.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.760
She said they've been through
two big holiday shopping

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.615
windows, which in retail,
is a really big deal.

00:03:01.615 --> 00:03:02.990
So I'm thrilled
that Google Cloud

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.360
can support retail customers.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.837
But by making her
people super productive,

00:03:07.837 --> 00:03:09.920
it's been really great for
Kohl's and really great

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:10.862
for her career.

00:03:10.862 --> 00:03:13.320
STEPHANIE WONG: And that's an
inspirational customer story.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.140
We also saw incredible downloads
during the keynote yesterday.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:17.557
Can you tell us
about some of them

00:03:17.557 --> 00:03:18.955
and which one was your favorite?

00:03:18.955 --> 00:03:21.330
ADAM SELIGMAN: There was a
couple that were a lot of fun.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:23.660
I really liked what Wicks
is doing with Google Cloud.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.360
They've gone from being like a
drag and drop web environment,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.240
but now it's
totally programmable

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.530
and extensible Google Cloud.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.660
That's really exciting.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.030
The opening demo that
Mandy Waite led us through.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.000
Her dev team is taking us
through was very, very cool.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.440
Because it was a
mix of developers

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.320
doing VM-based development,
with Jenkins for deployment

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.250
of new VM images, a
container-based app

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.040
in the middle, and then
extending with new services

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.910
with functions,
serverless functions,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:51.850
which is pretty cool.

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:54.560
So to see all those teams kind
of working together and using

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.410
tools like Cloud Code, our new
IDE integration for VS Code

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.010
IntelliJ, just awesome.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:01.760
People can work the
way they want to work.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.260
It all works together.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:04.850
All runs on Google
Cloud seamlessly.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.340
One pane of glass
with Stackdriver

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:10.198
to observe and manage it.

00:04:10.198 --> 00:04:11.240
And I think that's great.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.657
Let developers do what they
want and get to the fun parts.

00:04:13.657 --> 00:04:15.032
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's fantastic.

00:04:15.032 --> 00:04:17.660
Thank you so much again for
joining us today and giving us

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:18.952
